   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law, in relation\nto coupled entries",
    "basePrintNo": "S6390",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6390-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 54,
       "fullName": "Pamela Helming",
       "imgName": "773_Pamela_Helming.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 951,
       "sessionMemberId": 1812,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "HELMING"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §258, RWB L",
    "lawSection": "Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6390\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law, in\nrelation to coupled entries\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would end the mandatory coupling of entries in a race in which\nmarried jockeys ride.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law by\nadding a new section 258 to prohibit coupling of all horses trained or\nridden by a spouse, parent, issue or member of a jockey's household in\nthe betting with any horse ridden by such jockey.\n\nSection 2: establishes the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nGaming Commission regulation § 4040.2 currently limits women jockeys\nfrom fully pursuing their chosen career by mandating that all horses\ntrained or ridden by a spouse, parent, issue or member of a jockey's\nhousehold be coupled in the betting with any horse ridden by such jock-\ney. As as example, the rule has adversely impacted newly-married jock-\neys, Katie Davis and Trevor McCarthy, because it requires the NYRA\nRacing Office to couple their entries if both spouses ride in a race. No\nsuch mandate applies to brothers, nor does one exist in Maryland, where\nthe couple previously raced. The Gaming Commission's regulation does not\nimpact the husband's value in New York -- he gets plenty of races. In\ncontrast, she has not, despite coming into this season off two strong\nyears in Maryland. This rule is sexist and out-dated.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effective immediately.\n",
